# construction/fleet-claims.kyri -- the fleet's live claim board: what a ship is building, right now.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Absence is checkable and intent is not. `path_absence_scan.sh` answers
# whether a path stands here and upstream, and it answers about the remote AS IT STOOD WHEN ASKED
# (REDS %457). So a ship reads `absent`, builds for two hours, and comes back to find a peer's
# strictly wider instrument already landed -- with every word of its own reading still true.
#
# THAT IS NOT RARE. IT FIRED TWICE IN ONE DAY, ON TWO SHIPS. On `20260911` incense built
# `port_registry` -- 35 constants, 21 files, 44 pen legs, GREEN on metal -- and withdrew it whole,
# because `%715` had landed `port_band` two hours earlier. Bakery built a port census with a
# 29-leg pen the same day and dropped it into that same peer's scan, writing the sentence this
# board answers: *Nothing said the work was in flight.*
#
# WHAT A CLAIM IS. A declaration of intended work, published before the work exists -- the
# declaration half of `foundations/20260823-222019_what-brix-infuse-is.md` pointed at a fleet
# rather than at a host. It says how things SHOULD be and leaves the present moment alone, which is
# exactly what makes it readable, diffable, and reviewable with nothing built yet.
#
# WHAT A CLAIM IS NOT. Not a lock, and not a gate. Two ships may deliberately build one thing, and
# a reader that REFUSED would be a reader somebody turns off. It is also no promise that the work
# lands -- which is why a claim EXPIRES rather than standing until a hand clears it. A ship that
# dies mid-lap leaves a claim that goes quiet on its own, six hours on.
#
# THE READING THAT ACTUALLY CATCHES A DUPLICATE IS THE SENTENCE, NOT THE PATH. `port_registry` and
# `port_band` shared no path at all. Path overlap is the exact half and it would have missed the
# very case this board was built for; the `what` line is the half a person reads in ten seconds and
# gets right. So every check prints the whole live board, and the hand reads it.
#
# WHY THE BOARD HOLDS ONLY LIVE CLAIMS. This page is bounded at 24,576 bytes like every living pin,
# and a board that only grows is a board nobody reads. A landed claim's record is the commit; a
# withdrawn claim's record is its session log and, where it cost something, its REDS row. The
# history of this file is `git log` -- so clearing a closed claim off the board removes nothing
# that was ever testimony, and accrete-never-break is untouched.
#
# TWO TIME FIELDS, ANSWERING TWO QUESTIONS. `stamp` is the one-clock human record; `epoch` is UTC
# seconds, and age is read from it alone. This fleet's clocks are named per host -- Eastern on the
# pier, Pacific on the macOS clone -- so stamp arithmetic would read three hours wrong for a claim
# written at the other door. One field answering both questions would answer both badly.
#
# THE SHAPE is the standing roster's own: a record opens with `claim` and carries plain key-value
# lines until the next one. Read it with `tools/fixtures/f/fleet_claim_scan.sh` rather than by
# grepping here -- one parse for sh, for Rishi, and for a hand -- and note that the reader reads
# the ANOINTED REMOTE's copy rather than this one. A claim you have written and not pushed is a
# claim no peer can read; the reader says `board=local` when it meets that state, out loud.
#
#   sh tools/fixtures/f/fleet_claim_scan.sh --check <path> ...     # before you build
#   sh tools/f/fleet_claim.sh --open <name> --paths "..." --what "..."
#   git add construction/fleet-claims.kyri && git commit && git push xy
format fleet-claims-v1

# -- live claims -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The board opens empty, and the first claim belongs to the next ship that opens a build. Seeding
# it with this lap's own work would be a claim written after the fact, which is the one thing a
# declaration may never be.
