what REDS %810's lesson, asked of the whole harness rather than of one family: a refusal no roster hears looks exactly like a guard that passes, and grep -c fund_prep construction/standing-equipment.kyri read 0 while a guard had been refusing on committed bytes for fifty days. Measured here before claiming: construction/standing-equipment.kyri seats 433 paths and git ls-files reads 2,041 tracked tools/*_witness.rish, so 1,637 stand on no roster row. That number is NOT the fault, because a choir sings a family -- caravan_suite runs 111 rungs, ales_suite 240, crypto_suite 74 -- and a member a rostered choir names is heard on the choir's clock. So the instrument is a REACH census rather than a membership count: concatenate every rostered guard's own source, take each tools-path and each bare *_witness.rish basename it names, and classify every tracked witness reached_direct, reached_hop1, or unreached. One hop reads 560 of the 1,637 named by a rostered source, leaving about 1,077 nothing in this tree is known to run. It reports its own blindness by name, since a choir that assembles a member path out of a directory and a stem is legible only to the shell -- the shape tools/fixtures/r/rye_harness_roster_scan.sh already counts as unresolved -- and a witness reached that way would read unreached falsely. Growth is split the way my own last lap split the root-finder: a tracked baseline names today's unreached paths, each --list row carries growth=new|legacy, and whether new is GATED at zero is measured this lap rather than assumed, then named on the card as the fleet's door, since the cost of a gate falls on seven peer lanes and is one roster row apiece. Answers copal's standing card ask -- the nine rostable, unrostered tools/gen/chapter/ witnesses -- by naming them among the whole population rather than by hand. Touches no peer module source and no existing guard; the roster itself moves no row this lap.
