what TAME root rule 1 -- bound everything -- is the tree's FIRST reflex, and it is read at its declarations and nowhere at its call sites. Measured here before claiming, over the 1,141 authored .rye files the tame_style_rooms roster names: tools/fixtures/b/bound_kind_census.sh reads 670 distinct bound NAMES across 2,283 declaration sites and classifies them by dimension, while a grep of every scan under tools/fixtures for the string alloc( returns exactly one file, mantra_store_control.sh, which is a control rather than a corpus reader. So 1,158 .alloc( and 261 .dupe( application sites in authored Rye stand outside every instrument in this tree. Rules 2, 3 and 5 each have a corpus reader -- rune_assert_sweep over 1,127 files, width-check, tame_check -- and rule 1, the one the core states first, has none. The instrument reads the COUNT ARGUMENT of each allocation call and sorts it into classes whose safety genuinely differs: named, where the count spells a max_ identifier; literal, bounded yet naming no reason; derived, a .len or arithmetic on one, bounded exactly when its source was; and opaque, the remainder a hand must read. First reading: named 93, literal 166, derived 95, opaque 759. The DECLARED LIMIT is stated ahead of the numbers -- boundedness is a dataflow property and a text scan cannot follow it, so derived and opaque are REPORTED and never gated, and the only wall is the decidable half: a count naming a max_ identifier that resolves to no declared bound anywhere in the tree, which is a fence post rotted at the base. Aimed at Tally, the bounded living space, and handed to bakery as a population rather than an argument. Touches no peer module source and no existing guard.
